{
  "image_name": "0b7518defcb96e9330655d38e6ae54af.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how growth-factor and nutrient signaling converges on the mTOR pathway, and how this control is altered in cancer. At the membrane, growth factor receptors activate PI3K, IRS/GRB10, and AKT, which then regulate the TSC complex and mTOR. Two distinct complexes are shown: mTORC2, containing Rictor, which helps activate AKT and related kinases, and mTORC1, containing Raptor, which acts as the main hub for anabolic growth.\n\nmTORC1 integrates several inputs. Amino acids stimulate it, whereas low energy and cell stress act through TSC to restrain it. A negative-feedback loop from mTOR/S6K back toward upstream receptor signaling is also indicated. Downstream, mTORC1 promotes S6K and 4EBP/eIF4E signaling, driving ribosome production, mRNA translation, and lipid and nucleotide synthesis, while suppressing ULK1-mediated autophagy. AKT also inhibits FOXO, reducing transcription of growth factor receptor genes.\n\nThe lower part links this network to common oncogenic pathways. PTEN loss and PI3K–AKT activation, as well as RAS–RAF–MEK–ERK signaling, converge on TSC2 and mTORC1. ERK/RSK, MYC, and MNK further enhance translational output, emphasizing how multiple cancer pathways funnel into protein synthesis and cell growth.",
